  "client_view_text": " The 2008 financial crisis, also known as the global financial crisis, was a severe economic downturn that was triggered by a housing market bubble bursting in the United States. The crisis led to a global recession, widespread job losses, and a significant decline in economic output. The main causes of the crisis were: (1) excessive borrowing and lending, particularly in the subprime mortgage market, which led to a housing market bubble; (2) the securitization of these mortgages, which allowed banks to package and sell them to investors, creating a complex web of financial instruments; (3) the failure of regulatory bodies to monitor and regulate the financial sector; and (4) the excessive leverage and risk-taking by financial institutions, which made them vulnerable to collapse. The consequences of the crisis were severe, including: (1) a global recession, with GDP declining by 2.2% in 2009; (2) widespread job losses, with over 8 million jobs lost in the United States alone; (3) a significant decline in economic output, with the US GDP declining by 5.1% in 2009; and (4) a massive bailout of the financial sector, with the US government spending over $2 trillion to stabilize the system"
